and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\n- Consider the pair $(1,2)$ whose second component is $2.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $2:$ \n\n- Linking $(1,2)$ with $(2,1)$ requires that $(1,1)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Linking $(1,2)$ with $(2,3)$ requires that $(1,3)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pair $(1,3)$ whose second component is $3.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $3:$ \n\n- Linking $(1,3)$ with $(3,2)$ requires that $(1,2)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pair $(2,1)$ whose second component is $1.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $1:$ \n\n- Linking $(2,1)$ with $(1,2)$ requires that $(2,2)\\in R.$ However, this pair is *not* in $R.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nWe can stop checking since we have found a chain of pairs that do not satisfy the transitivity condition. Therefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince $(2,1)\\in R$ and $(1,2)\\in R$ but $(2,2)\\notin R,$ the relation is  $\\text{not transitive}.$" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186205242 1064 "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is **transitive** if, for all $x,y,z \\in A$, we have\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}x},\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y}) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y},{\\color{blue}z}) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad ({\\color{blue}x},{\\color{blue}z}) \\in R\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $({\\color{blue}x},\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y})$ and $(\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y},{\\color{blue}z})$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $({\\color{blue}x},{\\color{blue}z})$ should also belong to the relation.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The relation \"greater than\" ($\\gt$), defined over $\\mathbb{R}$, is transitive since ${\\color{blue}a} \\gt \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b} \\gt {\\color{blue}c}$ implies ${\\color{blue}a} \\gt {\\color{blue}c}$ for any integers $a$, $b$, and $c$.\n- The relation \"are congruent modulo $n$\" ($\\equiv$), defined over $\\mathbb{Z}$, is transitive since ${\\color{blue}a} \\equiv \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b} \\equiv {\\color{blue}c}$ implies ${\\color{blue}a} \\equiv {\\color{blue}c}$ for any integers $a$, $b$, and $c$, where $n$ is a fixed natural number.\n\nNotice from the definition of a transitive relation that if the first ordered pair is $(y,y)$, we have\n$(y,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (y,z) \\in R$\nwhich is always true.\n\nSimilarly, if the second ordered pair is $(y,y)$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,y) \\in R$\nwhich is always true.\n\nTherefore, to verify transitivity, it's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with\n\n$$\nx \\neq y\n$$\n\nand $(y,z)$ with\n\n$$\ny \\neq z\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139534303 17592186045417 false] [17592186205242 1064 "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is **transitive** if, for all $x,y,z \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}x},\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y}) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y},{\\color{blue}z}) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad ({\\color{blue}x},{\\color{blue}z}) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $({\\color{blue}x},\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y})$ and $(\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y},{\\color{blue}z})$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $({\\color{blue}x},{\\color{blue}z})$ should also belong to the relation.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The relation \"greater than\" ($\\gt$), defined over $\\mathbb{R},$ is transitive since ${\\color{blue}a} \\gt \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b} \\gt {\\color{blue}c}$ implies ${\\color{blue}a} \\gt {\\color{blue}c}$ for any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\n- The relation \"are congruent modulo $n$\" ($\\equiv$), defined over $\\mathbb{Z},$ is transitive since ${\\color{blue}a} \\equiv \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b} \\equiv {\\color{blue}c}$ implies ${\\color{blue}a} \\equiv {\\color{blue}c}$ for any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ where $n$ is a fixed natural number.\n\nNotice from the definition of a transitive relation that if the first ordered pair  is $(y,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n(y,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (y,z) \\in R\n$$\n\nwhich is always true.\n\nSimilarly, if the second ordered pair is $(y,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,y) \\in R\n$$\n\nwhich is always true. \n\nTherefore, to verify transitivity, it's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x \\neq y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y \\neq z.$\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186205248 1064 "Let's consider some \"nuanced\" cases.\n\n- For any set $A$, the universal relation $R = A^2$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n- The empty relation $R = \\emptyset$ is transitive. Since it does not contain any pairs, the antecedent in the implication from the definition of transitivity is always false:\n$\\underbrace{(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R}_{\\large \\textrm{false}} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R$\nTherefore, the implication is always true, and the relation is vacuously transitive.\n- The relation $R = \\{(1,2), (3,4)\\}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs. So, the antecedent in the implication is always false (similar to the empty case above), and therefore the implication is always true.\n- The relation $R=\\{(1,1), (2,2)\\}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs (i.e., it contains only reflexive pairs)." 13194139534303 17592186045417 false] [17592186205248 1064 "Let's consider some \"nuanced\" cases.\n\n- For any set $A,$ the universal relation $R = A^2$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R = \\emptyset$ is transitive. Since it does not contain any pairs, the antecedent in the implication from the definition of transitivity is always false:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R}_{\\large \\textrm{false}} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R\n$$\n\nTherefore, the implication is always true, and the relation is vacuously transitive.\n\n- The relation $R = \\{(1,2), (3,4)\\}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs. So, the antecedent in the implication is always false (similar to the empty case above), and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- The relation $R=\\{(1,1), (2,2)\\}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs (i.e., it contains only reflexive pairs)." 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186205253 1064 "A binary relation is transitive if and only if for every pair of \"linked\" arrows $x \\rightarrow y$ and $y \\rightarrow z$ in the corresponding digraph, the arrow $x \\rightarrow z$ also belongs to the graph. For example, the relation represented by the following digraph is transitive.\n\n![](images/96/96fe8200e2ebd03c23ade6b649a41345f75b66524c188d419ee12ea3c904942b.png)\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" arrows of the form $x \\to y$ with\n\n$$\nx \\neq y\n$$\n\nand $y \\to z$ with\n\n$$\ny \\neq z{:}\n$$\n\n- Consider the arrow $1 \\to 2$ that ends at $2$. It can be linked with the following arrows that starts at $2{:}$ Linking $1 \\to 2$ with $2 \\to 1$ requires that $1 \\to 1$ belongs to the graph, and this arrow is in the graph $\\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$Linking $1 \\to 2$ with $2 \\to 3$ requires 