2186418242 1075 #edb/ref 17592186418174 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418242 1075 #edb/ref 17592186418185 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418242 1075 #edb/ref 17592186418190 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418242 1075 #edb/ref 17592186418205 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418242 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418242 1077 "field-5" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418242 1078 #edb/ref 17592186418205 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418242 1079 #uuid "ef4800a4-4dd7-502d-8ed6-b7f58d1fc8fb" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418238 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418243 1069 "q-214430" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418243 1071 "Which of the following relations on the set $A={1,3,5,7}$ are transitive?\n\n- ${R}_{1}=∅$\n- ${R}_{2}={A}^{2}$\n- ${R}_{3}={(5,5)}$\n- ${R}_{4}={(5,1),(7,3)}$" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418243 1072 #uuid "202c6abf-dfb5-522a-9256-456835ce12bd" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418243 1074 "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\nMoreover, we recall the following:\n\n- The universal relation $R={A}^{2}$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R=∅$ is vacuously transitive since the antecedent in the implication is always false, and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- A relation containing no \"linked\" pairs is always transitive.\n\n- A relation containing only reflexive pairs is always transitive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations:\n\n- ${R}_{1}$ is transitive since it's the empty relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{2}$ is transitive since it's the universal relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{3}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{4}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\"" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418223 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418244 1069 "q-214431" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418244 1071 "Which of the following relations on the set $A={♢,♡,♠,♣}$ are transitive?\n\n- ${R}_{1}={(♢,♢),(♣,♣)}$\n- ${R}_{2}={(♠,♠)}$\n- ${R}_{3}={(♢,♠),(♡,♣)}$\n- ${R}_{4}=∅$" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418244 1072 #uuid "44cd3428-3b1c-5e19-9525-5e86c3ca616a" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418244 1074 "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\nMoreover, we recall the following:\n\n- The universal relation $R={A}^{2}$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R=∅$ is vacuously transitive since the antecedent in the implication is always false, and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- A relation containing no \"linked\" pairs is always transitive.\n\n- A relation containing only reflexive pairs is always transitive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations:\n\n- ${R}_{1}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{2}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{3}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{4}$ is transitive since it's the empty relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\"" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418229 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418232 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418235 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418237 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418245 1069 "q-213994" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418245 1071 "Let $R$ be a relation defined on the set $R$ as follows: \n\n$x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x+y=1$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}},\nbut \n{{field-3}},\nthe relation is \n{{field-4}}." 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418245 1072 #uuid "55431de3-ba18-5b2d-9929-1fa00aa2cc43" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418245 1074 "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nFor our relation, notice that\n\n- $(-2,3)\\in R$ since $-2+3=1,$ and\n\n- $(3,-2)\\in R$ since $3+(-2)=1,$ but\n\n- $(-2,-2)\\notin R$ since $-2+(-2)\\ne 1.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince $(-2,3)\\in R$ and $(3,-2)\\in R$ but \n$(-2,-2)\\notin R,$ the relation is $\\text{not transitive}.$" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418226 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418246 1069 "q-214065" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418246 1071 "![](images/1f/1fa323b54736f62bb334ae491d8d41deeba4a4e325f6e56f9651da147d50c349.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a transitive relation defined on the set $A={p,q,r}?$" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418246 1072 #uuid "64ea6724-f6be-5851-a399-860e0e1924d8" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418246 1074 "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have \"linked\" arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ then the arrow $x\\to z$ must be in the digraph too.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $q\\to r$ and $r\\to p,$ but has no arrow $q\\to p.$\n\n- Digraph II represents a transitive relation. For any pair of arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ the arrow  $x\\to z$ also belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $p\\to q$ and $q\\to r,$ but has no arrow $p\\to r.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418240 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418247 1069 "q-214002" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418247 1071 "Which of the following relations are transitive? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y,$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x+y<4,$ defined on $R$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4),$ defined on $Z$" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418247 1072 #uuid "70eb9024-0964-59ad-a103-f6948d25a78c" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418247 1074 "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is transitive. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,x)\\in R,$ then $(x,z)\\in R.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx∣y\\,\\text{and}\\,y∣z\\,⟹\\,x∣z\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y,z\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is not transitive. Notice that \n\n- $(1,-4)\\in S$ since $1+(-4)=-3<4,$ and\n\n- $(-4,5)\\in S$ since $-4+5=1<4,$ but\n\n- $(1,5)\\notin S$ since $1+5=6≮4.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is transitive. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in T$ and $(y,x)\\in T,$ then $(x,z)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,\\text{and}\\,y≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,⟹\\,x≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y,z\\in Z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ and $T$ only.\"" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418228 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418231 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418233 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418236 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418248 1069 "q-214053" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418248 1071 "![](images/20/2040dcca071aff2235916003d656ad12a9be20ae840124ae2152e5c48b4cccb1.png)\n\nA binary relation is represented by the digraph shown above. Select the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince the digraph has arrows\n{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}\nbut has no arrow\n{{field-3}},\nthe relation is \n{{field-4}}." 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418248 1072 #uuid "93be41a4-83b1-5ac1-a124-d1424d516f32" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true] [17592186418248 1074 "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have \"linked\" arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ then the arrow $x\\to z$ must be in the digraph too.\n\nExamining the diagram, we conclude the following:\n\nSince the digraph has arrows $1\\to 2$ and $2\\to 4$ but has no arrow $1\\to 4,$ the relation is $\\text{not transitive}.$" 13194139534303 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-01d07d82-bd5f-595d-b025-e9ac237f4946" 17592186418160 "answer-094ca2dc-5a28-5bd5-982c-a8839e78c987" 17592186418161 "answer-0c81ee74-4165-534e-af15-27d266abcfea" 17592186418162 "answer-0d89559c-8b93-5a56-8be7-a62e839644a8" 17592186418163 "answer-0f2f48f7-907c-5201-9e82-8318cf32ee9f" 17592186418164 "answer-11527144-b995-5941-ba03-c4baf29af809" 17592186418165 "answer-20f9e528-a322-5fab-b404-6979d866a63b" 17592186418166 "answer-2571c089-3c52-59d7-b473-6f9e0305f21e" 17592186418167 "answer-2a370020-7fc9-5273-b46a-bc1fe9f1427c" 17592186418168 "answer-2eb95cc2-4351-5219-bf66-5f1e95ed104c" 17592186418169 "answer-2f967206-082f-